From b4cbbd11e99966e0c1d5d4051fe6c945fff4a3ca Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Nguyen Minh Tien <zizuzacker@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 12 Jun 2026 13:22:08 +0700
Subject: [PATCH v2 2/2] iio: adc: Add TI ADS1220 driver

Add an IIO driver for the Texas Instruments ADS1220 24-bit delta-sigma
SPI ADC. The driver supports single-ended and differential voltage
channels described as device-tree child nodes, per-channel programmable
gain (exposed through scale) and data rate (exposed through sampling
frequency), the internal 2.048V reference, an external reference via a
regulator, or the analog supply (AVDD) as a ratiometric reference,
single-shot conversions and a DRDY-interrupt-driven triggered buffer.
Conversions are gated either on the DRDY interrupt or, when no interrupt
is wired, on a data-rate-derived delay. Runtime PM powers the device down
between conversions.

Signed-off-by: Nguyen Minh Tien <zizuzacker@gmail.com>
---
Changes in v2:
 - Rework scale handling: compute the available scales from the
   reference voltage at probe time as IIO_VAL_INT_PLUS_NANO values, and
   use the same table in read_raw, write_raw and read_avail, so reading
   the scale and writing the same value back round-trips. This also
   removes the division by a user-supplied value entirely (Jonathan,
   Sashiko).
 - Negate the shorted-input reading returned for IIO_CHAN_INFO_OFFSET
   to match the (raw + offset) * scale convention, and add comments
   referencing the datasheet's offset calibration section (Jonathan,
   Sashiko).
 - Use spi_write_then_read() with stack buffers for all transfers and
   drop the DMA-safe buffers from the state struct (Jonathan).
 - Add .validate_trigger = iio_validate_own_trigger: results may only
   be clocked out after DRDY and there is no other data-ready status to
   poll, so foreign triggers cannot be supported (Jonathan's question,
   Sashiko).
 - Use the PM_RUNTIME_ACQUIRE_IF_ENABLED_AUTOSUSPEND() guard in the
   single conversion path; the buffer path keeps explicit get/put since
   the reference is released in postdisable (Andy).
 - Send POWERDOWN in buffer postdisable so continuous mode does not
   keep running (and asserting DRDY) after the buffer is disabled,
   also with CONFIG_PM disabled (Sashiko).
 - Claim direct mode in write_raw so gain/data rate cannot change
   under an active buffer (Sashiko).
 - Only read the AVDD regulator voltage when AVDD is actually used as
   the reference; otherwise just enable it, so probing still works
   where the voltage cannot be read (Jonathan, Sashiko). Reject 0V
   readings from vref/avdd (Andy).
 - ads1220_datarate_to_code() returns -ENOENT for unknown rates and is
   reused for the data rate validation in write_raw (Andy).
 - Include what is used: array_size.h, bitops.h instead of bits.h,
   cleanup.h, math.h, minmax.h, time.h, types.h; use the
   USEC_PER_SEC/USEC_PER_MSEC/MSEC_PER_SEC constants for time values
   (Andy).
 - Drop pm_runtime_mark_last_busy() calls, now done by
   pm_runtime_put_autosuspend() itself (Jonathan, Andy).
 - Drop the runtime resume callback: register contents and the serial
   interface survive power-down and START/SYNC wakes the device
   (Jonathan).
 - Explain the post-RESET delay in terms of the 4.096MHz tCLK
   (Jonathan).
 - Fill the differential mux table completely with U8_MAX markers for
   the pairs the multiplexer cannot route, drop its first array
   dimension, and pass the input array to ads1220_map_mux() (Jonathan,
   Andy).
 - Use designated initializers for the channel spec, assign
   IIO_CHAN_SOFT_TIMESTAMP() directly, split declarations from
   assignments and use in_range() and ARRAY_SIZE() (Jonathan, Andy).
 - Use unsigned loop variables declared in the loops (Andy).
 - Drop the redundant bits_per_word = 8 assignment (Jonathan).
 - Drop the duplicate error message when requesting the IRQ fails
   (Andy).
 - Use a .name initializer in the spi_device_id table (Jonathan, Andy).
 - Comment why debugfs register access needs no runtime PM resume: the
   serial interface and registers stay functional in power-down. There
   is no regmap here, so the register range check is the validation of
   the user-supplied address (Sashiko, Andy's regmap question).
 - Raise the channel count limit from 7 to 12 to match the binding.
 - Add the driver F: line to the MAINTAINERS entry from patch 1
   (Jonathan).

